<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Tengo Jabavu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1859–1921</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Tengo Jabavu was a Xhosa journalist and political leader in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, educated at the Healdtown mission. In 1884 he founded Imvo Zabantsundu, the first newspaper owned and edited by a black South African, and he used the Cape's qualified franchise to organise African voters. He helped establish the South African Native College at Fort Hare, opened in 1916.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-tengo-jabavu.jpg" alt="John Tengo Jabavu" width="320" height="515" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Tengo_Jabavu_and_his_son_Davidson_Don_Tengo,_around_1903.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
